.TH "CSSMATHNEGATE.VALUE" "3JS" "August 11, 2026" "JavaScript" "JavaScript Reference Manual"
.SH NAME
CSSMathNegate.value \- CSSMathNegate: value property
.SH SYNOPSIS
\f[B]Note:\f[R] This feature is available in Web Workers.
.PP
The \f[B]\f[CB]value\f[B]\f[R] read\-only property of the
\f[CR]CSSMathNegate\f[R] interface returns the
\f[CR]CSSNumericValue\f[R] that is being negated.
.PP
This is the value passed to the constructor, rectified to a
\f[CR]CSSNumericValue\f[R] (if it isn\(cqt one already).
If a plain number was passed to the constructor the value returned by
this property is the passed value wrapped in a \f[CR]CSSUnitValue\f[R]
with \f[CR]unit: \(dqnumber\(dq\f[R].
.SH VALUE
A \f[CR]CSSNumericValue\f[R] or one of its derived types.
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS Basic usage
The following code creates a \f[CR]CSSMathNegate\f[R] object, then reads
its \f[CR]value\f[R].
.PP
In this case, we passed \f[CR]CSS.px(10)\f[R], so \f[CR]value\f[R] is a
\f[CR]CSSUnitValue\f[R].
Passing a composite expression such as
\f[CR]CSS.px(10).add(CSS.percent(5))\f[R] would result in
\f[CR]value\f[R] returning a \f[CR]CSSMathSum\f[R].
.IP
.EX
\f[B]const\f[R] negated = \f[B]new\f[R] CSSMathNegate(CSS.px(10));

console.log(negated.value); \f[I]// CSSUnitValue {value: 10, unit: \(dqpx\(dq}\f[R]
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]CSSMathInvert.value\f[R]
